#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:5
#: f4e2aa21543549b182770177f9ede246
msgid "Radio Failsafe"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:7
#: 39b1beb09d3140b698fd1e45611730e2
msgid "Copter supports several configurable failsafe options in cases where contact between the Pilot's RC transmitter and the autopilot's receiver is lost.  This page explains this failsafe's setup and testing.  Note the \"Radio failsafe\" was previously called \"Throttle failsafe\" because of the way in which some receivers use the throttle channel to signal the loss of contact."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:11
#: 183a400c5e2942db8654e78197289783
msgid "For Single Helicopter, Dual Helicopter and Quad Helicopter frames, Copter 3.6 and earlier in any :ref:`H_RSC Mode<H_RSC_MODE>` or Copter 4.0 in the RC Passthrough :ref:`H_RSC_Mode<H_RSC_MODE>` requires the RC receiver channel 8 to hold last value when transmitter signal is lost.  If the receiver sends no signal or does not hold the RC channel 8 value, the motor will be shutdown and the helicopter will crash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:18
#: b5f126d291e244fd8ee0853b02d8fc86
msgid "Copter also supports other failsafes, including: :ref:`Battery <failsafe-battery>`, :ref:`Ground Station <gcs-failsafe>` and :ref:`EKF/DCM failsafes <ekf-inav-failsafe>`. See the :ref:`failsafe-landing-page` for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:21
#: 6ba859407a3d44b9a15fe2ea133bc75c
msgid "When the failsafe will trigger"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:23
#: 7b79f3abb9df40eb924f29cce5bbf651
msgid "If enabled and set-up correctly the radio failsafe will trigger if any of these conditions occur for more than :ref:`RC_FS_TIMEOUT<RC_FS_TIMEOUT>` seconds (default = 1 sec):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:25
#: 2b032ceaca134670ae25d07639da1ace
msgid "The pilot turns off the RC transmitter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:26
#: fa3c93ae77b54e75ae87f776eb21ddfb
msgid "The vehicle travels outside of RC range and signal is lost."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:27
#: 661ac7769bcb4643a1c7b49648ca2501
msgid "RC_OVERRIDES are lost if :ref:`using a GCS only <common-gcs-only-operation>` is being used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:28
#: e9c2e717c0a843758e312e613c8d9a94
msgid "The receiver loses power (unlikely)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:29
#: 2c22a435f2b54ae2b83587035df3ba50
msgid "The wires connecting the receiver to the autopilot are broken (unlikely)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:32
#: 9a4bfa43e0a84843a574743b32308f94
msgid "The radio failsafe will also trigger if the pilot forces the throttle channel below :ref:`FS_THR_VALUE<FS_THR_VALUE>` from the transmitter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:35
#: 68b7aeac85fa4d6fbb217781da73651a
msgid "What will happen"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:37
#: 446b37d89ca740cfb8c9c84589268988
msgid "When a radio failsafe is triggered, the copter can be configured via parameters to do nothing, land immediately, RTL, or SmartRTL.  It can also be configured to bypass the failsafe in an Auto Mode mission, or to continue landing if already in a landing phase."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:39
#: 4db6204351964067a232ed60edfbed66
msgid "If the copter is disarmed, no failsafe will take place."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:40
#: 3095caed114e45dea1e8cd231873d6d8
msgid "If the copter is armed but has landed, the copter will immediately disarm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:41
#: b4042145a75746248b822a2da23e77c5
msgid "If the copter is armed in Stabilize or Acro modes, and the throttle input is at minimum, and the vehicle is not in :ref:`AirMode<airmode>` then the copter will immediately disarm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:42
#: 44a871dca8df45b3a2805c04b2f21ca1
msgid "Otherwise, the copter will take the actions as configured in the parameters described below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:44
#: b0d69dae301342a4af53609e1c2b622b
msgid "If the failsafe clears (i.e. transmitter and receiver regain contact) the copter will remain in its failsafe mode. It will **not** automatically return to the flight mode that was active before the failsafe was triggered. This means that if, for example, the vehicle was in Loiter when the failsafe occurred and the flight mode was automatically changed to RTL, even after the transmitter and receiver regained contact, the vehicle would remain in RTL.  If the pilot wished to re-take control in Loiter he/she would need to change your flight mode switch to another position and then back to Loiter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:47
#: 31b3232b16d84d2f87a8e91dd4ebfc49
msgid "Receiver Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:49
#: f0de7f405c524b63a5b181a0b41b6d75
msgid "By default, most newly purchased receivers will simply not output pulses if contact with the transmitter is lost. However, some low end receivers will be set-up to simply hold all channels at their last known position.  This is not good because the autopilot has no way to know that the Pilot has lost control of the vehicle.  Instead the receiver must be set-up to signal to the autopilot it has lost contact and there are two ways that it can do this (the method depends upon the receiver). Each brand of Transmitter/Receiver is slightly different so please refer to your transmitter's user manual to determine which method is available and how to set it up."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:52
#: 41f1be6675624238b6de8185056e3b44
msgid "Receiver configuration for low-throttle method"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:53
#: ee273db63ce446ccbfba7f8d22dbf923
msgid "The **\"Low-Throttle\" method** pulls the throttle channel (normally channel 3) to a value below the bottom of its normal range (normally below 1000us).  This method is used by Futaba systems and many older systems.  Below is the setup method for a Futaba T7C Transmitter with R617FS or TFR4-B receiver which uses the \"low throttle\" method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:59
#: 30a6452de4944714b703d5fa2293dd81
msgid "Many receivers allow for the failsafe positions to be set either by simply pressing a button on the receiver, or directly from the transmitter. In this case, the transmitter is temporarily adjusted to output a throttle signal below the normal low idle position (if low idle is 1000us, then 990uS would be sent) setting this as the failsafe value for the receiver, then adjusting low stick back to normal idle position. This below throttle idle value is then set for :ref:`FS_THR_VALUE<FS_THR_VALUE>`, as discussed below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:62
#: dbb698f49d6f4f109044cf6d2c91825b
msgid "Receiver configuration for No-Signal method"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:63
#: 063832b317ed4457a9cb9df734aa52dd
msgid "**\"No Signal\" method** - the receiver stops sending signals to the autopilot.  This is the preferred method and is how most modern FrSky receivers operate. Below is the setup method for a FlySky 9 channel transmitter with FrSky D4R-II receiver which uses the \"No Signal\" method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:70
#: 1e560d6706654661b21511b7de1437c3
msgid "Parameter Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:72
#: 57e07967af504a2199052f655d90db7d
msgid "The :ref:`FS_THR_ENABLE<FS_THR_ENABLE>` parameter can be set in the Mission Planner full parameter list or full parameter tree, or can also be set using the Mission Planner *failsafe options* dropdown in the Initial Setup >> Mandatory Hardware >> Failsafe menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:74
#: 2c326a119d864e709b53ba240ed0cbcb
msgid "**Disabled** (Value 0) will disable the radio failsafe entirely."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:75
#: f79acef1225545ad95b3159a0f5018cf
msgid "**Enabled Always RTL** (Value 1) will switch the copter to RTL Mode.  If the GPS position is not usable, the copter will change to Land Mode instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:76
#: 3262763404f34d898294fcbf9ea0e0c3
msgid "**Enabled Continue with Mission in Auto Mode** (this value has no effect in 4.0 and later with :ref:`FS_OPTIONS<FS_OPTIONS>` parameter replacing function, see below) (Value 2) will ignore the failsafe in an Auto Mode mission. Otherwise, it will behave the same as *Enabled Always RTL*. This option no longer exists in ArduCopter 4.0. Instead, see the :ref:`FS_OPTIONS<FS_OPTIONS>` parameter for this function. Setting this value in Copter 4.0 and later version will automatically be converted and set to (Value 1) and the :ref:`FS_OPTIONS<FS_OPTIONS>` will be modified to include bit (0) in the bitmask for \"Continue if in auto mode on Radio Failsafe\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:77
#: 853dee35de0e4cad9e6d608154dc0933
msgid "**Enabled Always Land** (Value 3) will switch the copter to Land Mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:78
#: 69ad8cf39c1b40f099e7e74807cde0ff
msgid "**Enabled SmartRTL or RTL** (Value 4) will switch the copter to SmartRTL mode. If SmartRTL is not available, the copter will switch to RTL Mode instead.  If the GPS position is not usable, the copter will change to Land Mode instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:79
#: d15e75036fbe4d1a9704b5ad5d71adee
msgid "**Enabled SmartRTL or Land** (Value 5) will switch the copter to SmartRTL mode. If SmartRTL is not available, the copter will switch to Land Mode instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:80
#: 225129ed1cce4d20999d93d798b46b40
msgid "**Enabled Auto DO_LAND_START or RTL** (value 6) will jump to the nearest \"DO_LAND_START\" mission item or RTL if no \"DO_LAND_START\" mission item has been programmed(see :ref:`common-do-land-start`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:81
#: b835bdb221ab4691bea6a7db09299e7f
msgid "**Enabled always Brake or Land** will BRAKE or LAND if GPS position is not available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:82
#: bd53a3b3cd6a49f98cf5a2966651b2dd
msgid "Any invalid value (Such as accidentally enter 99 as a parameter value) will will behave the same as **Enabled Always LAND**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:84
#: f2686302e1de4d9394d231877b3b1dd0
msgid "The :ref:`FS_THR_VALUE<FS_THR_VALUE>`  parameter can be set in the Mission Planner full parameter list or full parameter tree, or can also be set using the Mission Planner *FS PWM* value in the Initial Setup >> Mandatory Hardware >> Failsafe menu. -  At least 10 PWM higher than your Channel 3's PWM value when the throttle stick is fully down and the transmitter is **off** -  At least 10 PWM lower than your channel 3's PWM value when the throttle stick is fully down and the transmitter is **on** -  Above 910 PWM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:89
#: ecbe28751512401ab60e7069217145e4
msgid "The :ref:`FS_OPTIONS<FS_OPTIONS>`  parameter (Copter 4.0 and later) is a bitmask parameter to select one or more options that modify the standard actions of the radio, GCS, and battery failsafe. In the Mission Planner full parameter list or full parameter tree, the handy checkbox popup window is an easy what to set this (and any other bitmask) parameter. Be sure to go to Help > Check Beta Updates to pull the latest parameter definitions first while connected to the internet. The :ref:`FS_OPTIONS<FS_OPTIONS>` bits are as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:91
#: 09d7277f950347d3a255b70cfa198395
msgid "bit 0 set: Continue if in auto mode on :ref:`Radio Failsafe <radio-failsafe>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:92
#: 1e8b736c203a48c898e7281e70cf6e25
msgid "bit 1 set: Continue if in auto mode on :ref:`Ground Control Station Failsafe<gcs-failsafe>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:93
#: b1653921ed104a52a346e596e1ec0d85
msgid "bit 2 set: Continue if in guided mode :ref:`Radio Failsafe <radio-failsafe>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:94
#: 137c7c7e708a4ea2bd2b84d3236549ed
msgid "bit 3 set: Continue if landing on any failsafe"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:95
#: 4b5e1de835a240e6a524428667792f3f
msgid "bit 4 set: Continue in pilot control on :ref:`Ground Control Station Failsafe<gcs-failsafe>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:96
#: c6355079d98b48de9cbb9ad41963314d
msgid "bit 5 set: Release gripper during failsafe handling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:97
#: 0e67e2aaca7c4e56b918fcb8fa6f3eb9
msgid "if none of the above are set, then execute the :ref:`FS_THR_ENABLE<FS_THR_ENABLE>` option as configured."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:99
#: 75c1dabf4f80438998bbd23f1a5c4d0d
msgid "Only bitmask bits 0, 2, 3 & 5 affect actions taken during radio failsafe. This parameter also works in conjunction with the battery and GCS failsafe, so ensure you are taking all options into account when setting this parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:101
#: b04abaf1352e4c69aca685e37dc8537c
msgid "Below is a screenshot of the Mission Planner Initial Setup >> Mandatory Hardware >> Failsafe menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:107
#: 9078fab64c82473fa8674e75e786190f
msgid "Testing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:109
#: 1b90d8c72a7b4866835fa1033bd0cf37
msgid "You can check your failsafe by performing the following tests with the autopilot connected to the Mission Planner either via a USB cable or telemetry link. You can complete these tests without plugging in your LiPo battery but if you do connect a battery you should first remove the propellers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:115
#: c6600b5fec514cfcb1630544c60f1ed1
msgid "**Test #1 : if using the \"Low-Throttle\" method, ensure the throttle channel drops with loss of radio contact**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:118
#: 44d43c117cc847aebe10bdc4e31f411b
msgid "Ensure your RC transmitter is on and connected with the throttle all the way down and flight mode set to Stabilize"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:120
#: 1df698c25ba3440987c5bb2d4bf45f3b
msgid "The throttle (channel 3) PWM value should be approximately as in first illustration below.  Its value may be higher or lower but it should definitely be at least 10 higher than the value held in the FS PWM field"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:124
#: 7a78233fca874850ae211e16f19425ba
msgid "Turn the transmitter off and the throttle PWM value should drop to be at least 10 below the FS PWM field value (as in the second illustration below) below"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:131
#: 97bf53257008445a8f06d660da56bf5c
msgid "Test #2 : ensuring motors disarm if in STABILIZE or ACRO with throttle at zero"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:134
#: 8fa9d44e7a7c4172a90fcbe49fc7607b
msgid "Switch to stabilize mode, arm your motors but keep your throttle at zero. Turn off your transmitter. The motors should disarm immediately (red led will start flashing, DISARMED will be displayed in the Mission Planner's Flight Data screen)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:139
#: 4a4a8f97e9c24ea68e4a432b992898c7
msgid "Test #3 : ensuring flight mode changes to RTL or LAND when throttle is above zero"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:142
#: 3d49dc7cb725410681dd49d392360d3f
msgid "Switch to stabilize mode, arm your motors and raise your throttle to the mid point. Turn off your transmitter. The Flight Mode should switch to RTL if you have a GPS lock or LAND if you do not have a GPS lock (the flight mode and GPS lock status are visible in the Mission Planner's flight data screen)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:148
#: 6ce46baafd564266b73f576f8a3df39a
msgid "Test #4 : retaking control after the failsafe has cleared"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:150
#: 85d7e24e235344ff91df70c478d7b73a
msgid "continuing on from test #3, turn your transmitter back on"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:151
#: 80e095f1d8ac4e8f85af05afe3b6caf3
msgid "while the flight mode is still in RTL or LAND and armed, change the flight mode switch to another position and then back to stabilize mode.  Ensure that the flight mode displayed on the Failsafe page is updating appropriately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:156
#: 268b5c8ada9b46bf802d0225e40d779c
msgid "Test #5 (optional) : removing power from the receiver"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:158
#: 962307a75c8e426eb03f5f4c98bbbe9f
msgid "Switch to stabilize mode, arm your motors and keep your throttle above zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:160
#: 2080936d7cef4582b7af8c512a7439ff
msgid "Carefully disconnect the power wires connecting the receiver to the autopilot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:162
#: eb1505bb9c234c00a37c3c5ff579b7a6
msgid "The Flight Mode should switch to RTL or LAND as described in Test #3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:164
#: 9eaf85f8c73f48fd931c192e64d51b03
msgid "Unplug the autopilot so that it is powered down before reattaching the receiver's power"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:168
#: 68102706e01a4c498f415011ff2a1d2d
msgid "Using the receiver to set the flight mode (**DO NOT USE!**)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:170
#: 1754ad25dff44f119ad882c3d51e4ece
msgid "Instead of setting up the receiver and autopilot as described above (i.e. \"Low-Throttle\" and \"No Signal\" methods) the receiver can be set-up to set channel 5 (flight mode channel) to a :ref:`flight mode <flight-modes>` slot that has been set to RTL.  For example the receiver could be setup to move ch5's pwm value to 1700us which is \"Flight Mode 5\" which could then be set to RTL on the Mission Planner's Initial Setup >> Mandatory Hardware >> Flight Modes screen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/radio-failsafe.rst:178
#: 31a34645ee7a47b7900be3356a7b8e23
msgid "Although this appears to work it is strongly not recommended because it can result in situations that can lead to a crash. Since the autopilot has no way of knowing that an RC failsafe has occurred, undesired behavior can occur such as overriding other failsafes like low battery or GCS failsafes with mode changes."
msgstr ""
